Assembler Version 12
====================
Assembled by: version 11 (both instgen and asm)

Features Supported by This Assembler
-------------------------------------
Same features as version 11. No new assembler capabilities. The significance
of this version is that the source code itself is rewritten in standard 6502
syntax, taking advantage of version 11's standard syntax support.

Version 12 produces binary-identical output to version 11 for any given input.
This is the bootstrap chain's standard syntax migration: two implementations
(non-standard source assembled by v10, standard source assembled by v11)
producing the same binary.

Syntax (standard 6502 - same as version 11):
- Standard 6502 mnemonic syntax: LDA #$42, STA $00, LDA ($10),Y, etc.
- Automatic addressing mode detection from operand syntax
- Automatic zero page detection: operand < $100 uses zero page mode
- All 13 standard 6502 addressing modes
- Character literals: #'A', #'\n', #'\\', #'\''
- DATA pseudo-instruction
- Hex constants: $xx (1 byte) or $xxxx (2 bytes)
- Quoted strings with \n and \\ escapes
- Label definitions with PC value or explicit assignment
- PC setting via: * = $xxxx
- Low byte (<) and high byte (>) operators on labels
- Comments with semicolons
- Two-pass assembly
- .include directive
- .zeropage and .code section directives
- Command-line arguments: <assembler> <input> <output> [debug]
- Local labels with . prefix, scoped to enclosing global label

Directives:
- .include filename
- .zeropage - switches to zero page section
- .code - switches back to code section

Full 6502 instruction set: same as version 11 (56 opcodes plus DATA pseudo)

Error handling: same as version 11 (errors 1-12, 13, 16)

New in this version (relative to version 11):
- No new assembler features. The assembler produces binary-identical output
  to version 11 for any given input.
- This version exists solely as a source code migration: the assembler's own
  source is rewritten from non-standard to standard 6502 syntax.

Features Used in This Assembler's Source Code
----------------------------------------------
- Standard 6502 mnemonic syntax (this is the milestone change):
  - LDA #$xx (immediate) instead of LDA# $xx
  - LDA $xx / STA $xx (auto zero page) instead of LDAZ / STAZ
  - LDA ($xx),Y (indirect indexed) instead of LDAZ(),Y $xx
  - LDA $xxxx,X (absolute indexed) instead of LDA,X $xxxx
  - BIT $xx instead of BITZ $xx
  - INC $xx instead of INCZ $xx
  - ORA $xx instead of ORAZ $xx
  - EOR $xx instead of EORZ $xx
  - ASL A (accumulator) instead of ASLA
  - CMP #$xx instead of CMP# $xx
- Character literals: #'x', #'\n', #'\\', #'\''
- DATA pseudo-instruction
- DATA $00 for zero page variable allocation in .zeropage section
- Hex constants ($xx and $xxxx)
- Quoted strings with escape sequences
- Labels and label assignments (label = value)
- PC setting (* = $2000 and * = $FFFC for vectors)
- Low byte (<) and high byte (>) operators
- Labels as absolute 2-byte operands
- Comments (;)
- BRK on its own line, followed by DATA $xx "message" $00 on the next line
  (instead of BRK $xx "message" $00 on one line)
- .include directive (out/inst.asm.out, environment.asm, common.asm,
  file_stack.asm, to_decimal.asm, fwdref.asm)
- .zeropage and .code section directives
- Local labels (.label syntax)
- Command-line arg access: argc, argv
- File I/O: open, close, read, openout, write

New syntax used in source (relative to version 11):
- Standard 6502 mnemonic syntax replaces non-standard syntax throughout.
  This is a major milestone: from this version forward, all source code
  uses standard assembly syntax.
- #'x' character literal syntax
- BRK as implied instruction (on its own line) with DATA on next line,
  replacing BRK with inline data

Other source changes:
- All included modules (common.asm, hash_table.asm, file_stack.asm,
  to_decimal.asm) also rewritten in standard syntax
- instgen.asm rewritten in standard syntax but generates the same
  instruction table format as version 11
